commit 6909b3dfbbed786df626e347bff4da2cf79d400a
Author: Sully Sullenberger <sully@sadburger.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 24 12:18:23 2026 -0700

    Serve a web control surface from the remote port, paired from a URL whose fragment carries the code

    The port serves three embedded files (web/index.html, app.js, app.css)
    answered on the connection thread with a strict Content-Security-Policy
    and Connection: close. A phone opens the pairing window's QR, the page
    compares the key with /info, pairs with "transport": "cookie", lists the
    sessions with the waiting ones first, reads a Claude Code session's last
    response or any other session's screen, and sends a reply under control.
    It polls /waiting and treats the events socket as the faster path.

    Contract additions:
    - No new AppAction or Effect variant, no port method.
    - RemoteReply.headers, RemoteReply::new; RemoteRequest.{cookie, origin, host}.
    - core::remote::invite_url (re-exported as core::invite_url).
    - adapters::web (PAGES, CSP, PAGE_HEADERS, page); adapters::remote::
      {token_cookie_name, cookie_value, PAGE_PER_MINUTE}.
    - Wire: GET /api/v1/me; /pair request field transport; the
      __Host-sb-<server_id> cookie accepted as a credential beside the
      bearer header; the 403 code origin; GET /, /app.js, /app.css before
      pairing; the invite text is a URL (the JSON still parses in the Swift
      client).
    - No change to model.rs, control/, or any schema version.

    Spike 23 (browser cookie, two instances' cookies, wss under the CSP,
    connections per load) is written but not run: no browser could be
    driven, so acceptance steps 1 to 4 rest on the owner's phone.

